      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Perturbing an impostor plane toward a PCA-derived best-fitting plane reduces viewpoint-change popping for walking human impostors while limiting newly introduced distortions.",
      "short_title": "PCA planes reduce popping",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Starting from an impostor plane purely perpendicular to the camera, we “perturbed” its orientation using the resulting plane obtained from a PCA of the sample image. This minimized the popping while limiting the introduction of other artifacts."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Packing each prerendered human sample into the smallest containing rectangle reduces the texture memory needed for human images to one quarter of the original value without loss of image quality.",
      "short_title": "Sample packing cuts memory",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Thanks to this process, the resulting new image is much smaller than the original without any loss of image quality.",
        "With our current reorganization strategy, we can reduce the amount of texture memory necessary to store our human images to 25 percent of the original value."
