  "source_dossier": {
    "summary_short": "This source presents an image-based rendering pipeline for animated crowds in virtual cities. It combines optimized impostors, texture packing, compression, alpha-guided recoloring, and grid-based behavior support. The implementation reports 10,000 varied humans in a 41,260-polygon village at interactive frame rates.",
    "summary_long": "The article addresses a specific graphics bottleneck: populated virtual cities require both large static environments and many animated human agents, but fully polygonal crowds make real-time display expensive. Tecchia, Loscos, and Chrysanthou therefore use image-based impostors for distant humans, exploiting the typical viewing conditions of urban walkthroughs where many people are visible but not necessarily close to the camera. Their approach is not just a generic billboard substitution. They tune the impostor representation for walking human geometry, reduce the texture-memory footprint of prerendered samples, and use multipass rendering to create visual variety from a limited set of human models. The technical path is pragmatic: reduce the number of polygons per person, retain enough directional and animation sampling to avoid severe artifacts, and spend saved memory on more types and appearances. Several pieces of evidence anchor the proposal. A PCA-informed plane-selection argument explains how the system reduces viewpoint-change popping without fully adopting a distortion-prone best-fitting plane. Packing each sample into its smallest rectangle cuts memory to 25 percent of the original sample layout, and OpenGL texture compression further reduces frame storage while retaining alpha data for later recoloring. Alpha-channel masks then support multipass rendering, changing clothes and other body regions while preserving the baked shading in the source texture. The implementation also integrates a grid for collision and shadowing, and reuses impostor silhouettes to create posture-aware shadows with one additional polygon. The benchmark demonstrates the intended scale: six avatar types, 10,000 different humans, a 41,260-polygon village, and measured frame rates that remain interactive despite falling as crowd size grows. The source is strongest as a systems paper showing how several approximate techniques combine into a usable real-time renderer; its limits are equally clear, including assumptions about camera distance, sunlight-like illumination, synthetic source models, and the need for culling and richer behavior in future work.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Rendering Problem",
        "body": "The source frames crowd rendering as the intersection of two expensive tasks: navigating a polygon-heavy city and animating many human agents. Its key proposition is that distant avatars do not need full polygonal treatment when image-based impostors can preserve plausible appearance at interactive rates. This motivates the entire pipeline rather than treating texture compression or recoloring as isolated optimizations.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "0",
          "7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Impostor Plane Choice",
        "body": "The most specific rendering-quality argument concerns popping when the viewpoint changes. A conventional plane perpendicular to the sampling camera ignores object shape, while the full PCA best-fit plane can introduce distortions. The compromise is to perturb the conventional plane toward the PCA-derived orientation, reducing visible popping for human impostors while keeping artifacts bounded.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Texture Memory Strategy",
        "body": "The memory strategy has two layers. First, symmetric walking motion reduces directional samples, and rectangle packing removes unused texture space around each prerendered human. Second, OpenGL compression provides a further 1:4 reduction and preserves alpha values, making a multi-type, multi-frame human library plausible within the cited 25 Mbyte budget.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "2",
          "3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Avatar Variety Mechanism",
        "body": "Visual variety comes from recoloring regions rather than generating a fully distinct model for every person. Alpha-channel masks identify body regions such as clothes, hair, and skin; multipass rendering then modulates selected regions with different colors. The source is careful to note a trade-off: additional passes and regions can improve variety but may reduce rendering speed.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Behavior and Shadows",
        "body": "The renderer is paired with simple environmental state. Regular floor tiles encode collision and shadow information, giving agents local movement decisions and preventing occupied-destination conflicts. The impostor database is also reused to project human shadows, which adds environmental visual integration at only one extra polygon per avatar shadow.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "5",
          "6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Benchmark Interpretation",
        "body": "The reported results support the system-level claim but should be read with their constraints. On an 800-MHz Pentium III with a GeForce GTS2, the system displays 10,000 humans in a 41,260-polygon village at 12 to 20 frames per second. The frame-rate series also shows that the static city already costs much of the budget and that crowd scaling is close to linear in time per frame.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "7",
          "0"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Assumptions and Limits",
        "body": "The method assumes viewers remain far enough from avatars for orthographic impostors and limited texture dimensions to hold up. It also assumes effectively sun-like lighting for shadow reuse and relies on synthetic models rather than captured humans. The authors identify hybrid polygonal close-ups, normal-map shading, culling, photographic acquisition, and richer behavior as future improvements.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "1",
          "6",
          "7"
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
