{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"find verification/example_site/spaces/graphics/sources -maxdepth 2 -type f | sort | sed -n '1,40p'; printf '\\\\nsource records:\\\\n'; for f in verification/example_site/spaces/graphics/sources/*/*.json verification/example_site/spaces/graphics/sources/*.json; do [ -f \\\"\"'$f\" ] && printf '\"'\\\\n--- %s ---\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\" && sed -n '\"'1,160p' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"verification/example_site/spaces/graphics/sources/records/source-01-sphere-tracing-a-geometric-method-for-the-antialiased-ray-tracing-of-implicit-surfaces--eee351440d34.json\nverification/example_site/spaces/graphics/sources/records/source-02-image-based-crowd-rendering--565ae2b0e3a1.json\n\nsource records:\n\n--- verification/example_site/spaces/graphics/sources/records/source-01-sphere-tracing-a-geometric-method-for-the-antialiased-ray-tracing-of-implicit-surfaces--eee351440d34.json ---\n{\n  \"analysis_policy\": {\n    \"fallback_artifacts\": [\n      \"source_file\"\n    ],\n    \"preferred_artifact\": \"source_markdown\",\n    \"quality_status\": \"usable\",\n    \"warnings\": []\n  },\n  \"article_kind\": null,\n  \"artifact_root\": \"sources/artifacts/source-01-sphere-tracing-a-geometric-method-for-the-antialiased-ray-tracing-of-implicit-surfaces--eee351440d34\",\n  \"artifacts\": {\n    \"front_page_image\": \"sources/artifacts/source-01-sphere-tracing-a-geometric-method-for-the-antialiased-ray-tracing-of-implicit-surfaces--eee351440d34/front_page.png\",\n    \"source_extraction\": \"sources/artifacts/source-01-sphere-tracing-a-geometric-method-for-the-antialiased-ray-tracing-of-implicit-surfaces--eee351440d34/source_extraction.json\",\n    \"source_file\": \"sources/artifacts/source-01-sphere-tracing-a-geometric-method-for-the-antialiased-ray-tracing-of-implicit-surfaces--eee351440d34/source.pdf\",\n    \"source_markdown\": \"sources/artifacts/source-01-sphere-tracing-a-geometric-method-for-the-antialiased-ray-tracing-of-implicit-surfaces--eee351440d34/source.md\",\n    \"source_provenance\": \"sources/artifacts/source-01-sphere-tracing-a-geometric-method-for-the-antialiased-ray-tracing-of-implicit-surfaces--eee351440d34/source_provenance.md\"\n  },\n  \"authors\": [\n    \"Florine Fouquart\",\n    \"Chu-Yin Chen\"\n  ],\n  \"citation_count\": null,\n  \"citation_count_as_of\": null,\n  \"citation_count_confidence\": \"unknown\",\n  \"citation_count_provider\": \"unknown\",\n  \"date\": null,\n  \"display_title\": \"Shadercoding Turns Mathematics Into Live Digital Art\",\n  \"external_related_links\": [\n    {\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"domain\": \"doi.org\",\n      \"link_type\": \"canonical_paper\",\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"origin\": \"reference_doi\",\n        \"source_field\": \"references[1].doi\"\n      },\n      \"quality_status\": \"trusted\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Derived from a DOI found in the source references.\",\n      \"title\": \"Virtuelle 12 6 pp.527-545. \\u27e8 \\u27e9. \\u27e8hal-04421881\\u27e9\",\n      \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1007/s003710050084\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ingested_at\": \"2026-04-26T16:31:44Z\",\n  \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n  \"references\": [\n    {\n      \"arxiv\": null,\n      \"authors\": [],\n      \"doi\": null,\n      \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"url\": \"https://univ-paris8.hal.science/hal-04421881v1\",\n      \"year\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"arxiv\": null,\n      \"authors\": [],\n      \"doi\": \"10.1007/s003710050084\",\n      \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n      \"title\": \"Virtuelle 12 6 pp.527-545. \\u27e8 \\u27e9. \\u27e8hal-04421881\\u27e9\",\n      \"url\": null,\n      \"year\": 2019\n    }\n  ],\n  \"related_link_enrichment\": {\n    \"skip_reason\": null,\n    \"sources\": [\n      \"references\"\n    ],\n    \"status\": \"enriched\",\n    \"warnings\": [\n      \"Skipped 1 unsupported external-link candidate(s).\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"schema_version\": \"source_record_v1\",\n  \"source_date_inference\": {\n    \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n    \"date\": \"2019\",\n    \"origin\": \"source_markdown_citation\",\n    \"rationale\": \"The extracted citation line names Arts et Technologies de l\\u2019Image, Images num\\u00e9riques et R\\u00e9alit\\u00e9 Virtuelle, 2019, 12(6), pp.527-545; the HAL deposit date is later and is not treated as publication date.\"\n  },\n  \"source_dossier\": {\n    \"sections\": [\n      {\n        \"body\": \"The source defines shadercoding through the shader itself: a GPU-directed program involved in the rendering pipeline. Its taxonomy of vertex, tessellation, geometry, and fragment shaders gives readers a concrete account of where code enters image production. This matters because the later art argument depends on treating code as the actual medium of visual formation, not as a hidden support tool.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n          \"1\",\n          \"2\"\n        ],\n        \"heading\": \"Technical Object Of Study\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"body\": \"The authors emphasize fragment shaders because they convert pixel positions into colors and can use formulas, time variables, and external inputs to generate animated images. That framing makes shadercoding legible as a generative practice: the image is computed from mathematical relations, and interaction can be incorporated into the same visual procedure.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n          \"2\",\n          \"0\"\n        ],\n        \"heading\": \"Fragment Shaders As Generators\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"body\": \"Raymarching is presented as the technique that lets fragment shaders recover 3D depth. The source explains the procedure in terms of distance functions, rays launched per pixel, stepwise traversal, and encounters with objects described by signed distance fields. This supports the claim that mathematical scene descriptions can produce complex real-time visual spaces.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n          \"3\",\n          \"4\"\n        ],\n        \"heading\": \"Raymarching And Spatial Depth\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"body\": \"The article repeatedly rejects a simple opposition between technical constraint and creative expression. Mathematical fluency, spatial reasoning, vector operations, and shader language mastery are treated as conditions of artistic agency. In this account, the artist gains expressive freedom not by avoiding technical systems, but by understanding them deeply enough to improvise inside them.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n          \"4\",\n          \"5\",\n          \"8\"\n        ],\n        \"heading\": \"Technique As Artistic Condition\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"body\": \"Shader Showdowns supply the article's clearest institutional performance frame: a public, timed event where participants code a shader effect in roughly half an hour and are judged on image, code technique, and visual evolution. The visibility of writing code makes the procedure itself part of the artwork's reception, alongside the generated image.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n          \"6\",\n          \"7\"\n        ],\n        \"heading\": \"Live Coding As Performance Frame\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"body\": \"The source is strongest as conceptual and practice-based analysis. Its evidence includes technical definitions, procedural descriptions, cited aesthetic theory, event formats, and first-person accounts from Revision 2016 and 2018. It does not provide quantitative audience data or comparative studies, so broad claims about digital-art performance should be read as argued interpretation rather than measured generalization.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n          \"5\",\n          \"6\",\n          \"7\",\n          \"8\"\n        ],\n        \"heading\": \"Evidence And Limits\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"summary_long\": \"Florine Fouquart and Chu-Yin Chen present shadercoding as a practice located at the junction of GPU programming, mathematics, computer graphics, and performance art. The article begins with a technical introduction: shaders are GPU programs that participate in rendering, fragment shaders map pixel positions to colors, and mathematical formulas can generate patterns, animation, and interactivity. Raymarching receives particular attention because it lets a fragment shader recover three-dimensional depth by casting rays through distance-field descriptions of space, making complex forms and repeated spaces available in real time. This technical exposition is not an end in itself. The authors use it to question the figure of the digital artist as a technical artist whose aesthetic choices are formed through scientific and mathematical processes. The article then shifts into a practice-based reflection on live coding, especially Shader Showdown events where participants write visual shader effects in 25 or 30 minutes before an audience. The source argues that constraint, visibility, and time pressure can become generative. Rather than separating technique from expression, the authorial account describes technical mastery as the condition that allows improvisation, bodily absorption, and dialogue with spectators. The strongest evidence is explanatory and experiential: a shader pipeline taxonomy, descriptions of fragment-shader and raymarching procedures, a concrete vector-math lighting example, the judging structure of Shader Showdowns, and a first-person account of reaching the Revision 2018 final. The result is a source best read as theoretical-practical commentary rather than empirical validation. It establishes a vocabulary for understanding shadercoding as art, but its general claims about performance rest on reflective experience, cited aesthetic theory, and examples from live-coding culture rather than systematic audience or practitioner study.\",\n    \"summary_short\": \"Fouquart and Chen frame shadercoding as both GPU graphics technique and live digital-art practice. They explain fragment shaders, raymarching, distance fields, and mathematical image generation, then connect those techniques to Shader Showdown performance. The source argues that technical mastery and constraint can enable expressive real-time improvisation.\"\n  },\n  \"source_extraction\": {\n    \"converter_name\": \"pdftotext\",\n    \"converter_version\": null,\n    \"quality_status\": \"usable\",\n    \"status\": \"success\",\n    \"warnings\": []\n  },\n  \"source_id\": \"source-01-sphere-tracing-a-geometric-method-for-the-antialiased-ray-tracing-of-implicit-surfaces--eee351440d34\",\n  \"source_kind\": \"file\",\n  \"source_locator\": \"01-sphere-tracing-a-geometric-method-for-the-antialiased-ray-tracing-of-implicit-surfaces.pdf\",\n  \"source_media_type\": \"application/pdf\",\n  \"source_semantic\": {\n    \"authors\": [\n      \"Florine Fouquart\",\n      \"Chu-Yin Chen\"\n    ],\n    \"display_title\": \"Shadercoding Turns Mathematics Into Live Digital Art\"\n  },\n  \"source_type\": \"file\",\n  \"summary\": \"Fouquart and Chen frame shadercoding as a digital-art practice where mathematical image generation, GPU rendering technique, and live coding performance converge. The source introduces shader mechanics, emphasizes raymarching and fragment shaders, and argues from practice that technical constraints and mastery can enable real-time artistic improvisation.\",\n  \"title\": \"01-sphere-tracing-a-geometric-method-for-the-antialiased-ray-tracing-of-implicit-surfaces\",\n\n--- verification/example_site/spaces/graphics/sources/records/source-02-image-based-crowd-rendering--565ae2b0e3a1.json ---\n{\n  \"analysis_policy\": {\n    \"fallback_artifacts\": [\n      \"source_file\"\n    ],\n    \"preferred_artifact\": \"source_markdown\",\n    \"quality_status\": \"usable\",\n    \"warnings\": []\n  },\n  \"article_kind\": null,\n  \"artifact_root\": \"sources/artifacts/source-02-image-based-crowd-rendering--565ae2b0e3a1\",\n  \"artifacts\": {\n    \"front_page_image\": \"sources/artifacts/source-02-image-based-crowd-rendering--565ae2b0e3a1/front_page.png\",\n    \"source_extraction\": \"sources/artifacts/source-02-image-based-crowd-rendering--565ae2b0e3a1/source_extraction.json\",\n    \"source_file\": \"sources/artifacts/source-02-image-based-crowd-rendering--565ae2b0e3a1/source.pdf\",\n    \"source_markdown\": \"sources/artifacts/source-02-image-based-crowd-rendering--565ae2b0e3a1/source.md\",\n    \"source_provenance\": \"sources/artifacts/source-02-image-based-crowd-rendering--565ae2b0e3a1/source_provenance.md\"\n  },\n  \"authors\": [\n    \"Franco Tecchia\",\n    \"C\\u00e9line Loscos\",\n    \"Yiorgos Chrysanthou\"\n  ],\n  \"citation_count\": null,\n  \"citation_count_as_of\": null,\n  \"citation_count_confidence\": \"unknown\",\n  \"citation_count_provider\": \"unknown\",\n  \"date\": null,\n  \"display_title\": \"Image-Based Crowd Rendering for Virtual Cities\",\n  \"external_related_links\": [],\n  \"ingested_at\": \"2026-04-26T16:38:44Z\",\n  \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n  \"references\": [\n    {\n      \"arxiv\": null,\n      \"authors\": [],\n      \"doi\": null,\n      \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n      \"title\": \"Techniques Springer Computer Science New York topic please visit our Digital Library at\",\n      \"url\": \"http://computer\",\n      \"year\": 2000\n    },\n    {\n      \"arxiv\": null,\n      \"authors\": [],\n      \"doi\": null,\n      \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n      \"title\": \"samples on a texture using a regular grid.1 Each sample compressed format /\",\n      \"url\": \"http://oss.sgi.com/projects\",\n      \"year\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"arxiv\": null,\n      \"authors\": [],\n      \"doi\": null,\n      \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n      \"title\": \"s3tc compression source\",\n      \"url\": \"http://oss.sgi.com/projects/ogl-sam-\",\n      \"year\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"arxiv\": null,\n      \"authors\": [],\n      \"doi\": null,\n      \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n      \"title\": \"project at University College London. See . ronments: From Realism to Real Time Addison Wesley\",\n      \"url\": \"http://www\",\n      \"year\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"arxiv\": null,\n      \"authors\": [],\n      \"doi\": null,\n      \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n      \"title\": \"9 A closer view algorithms. Visit\",\n      \"url\": \"http://www.cs.ucl\",\n      \"year\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"related_link_enrichment\": {\n    \"skip_reason\": \"no_curated_links_found\",\n    \"sources\": [\n      \"references\"\n    ],\n    \"status\": \"skipped\",\n    \"warnings\": [\n      \"Skipped 5 unsupported external-link candidate(s).\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"schema_version\": \"source_record_v1\",\n  \"source_date_inference\": {\n    \"confidence\": \"unknown\",\n    \"date\": null,\n    \"origin\": \"unknown\",\n    \"rationale\": null\n  },\n  \"source_dossier\": {\n    \"sections\": [\n      {\n        \"body\": \"The source frames crowd rendering as the intersection of two expensive tasks: navigating a polygon-heavy city and animating many human agents. Its key proposition is that distant avatars do not need full polygonal treatment when image-based impostors can preserve plausible appearance at interactive rates. This motivates the entire pipeline rather than treating texture compression or recoloring as isolated optimizations.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n          \"0\",\n          \"7\"\n        ],\n        \"heading\": \"Rendering Problem\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"body\": \"The most specific rendering-quality argument concerns popping when the viewpoint changes. A conventional plane perpendicular to the sampling camera ignores object shape, while the full PCA best-fit plane can introduce distortions. The compromise is to perturb the conventional plane toward the PCA-derived orientation, reducing visible popping for human impostors while keeping artifacts bounded.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n          \"1\"\n        ],\n        \"heading\": \"Impostor Plane Choice\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"body\": \"The memory strategy has two layers. First, symmetric walking motion reduces directional samples, and rectangle packing removes unused texture space around each prerendered human. Second, OpenGL compression provides a further 1:4 reduction and preserves alpha values, making a multi-type, multi-frame human library plausible within the cited 25 Mbyte budget.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n          \"2\",\n          \"3\"\n        ],\n        \"heading\": \"Texture Memory Strategy\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"body\": \"Visual variety comes from recoloring regions rather than generating a fully distinct model for every person. Alpha-channel masks identify body regions such as clothes, hair, and skin; multipass rendering then modulates selected regions with different colors. The source is careful to note a trade-off: additional passes and regions can improve variety but may reduce rendering speed.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n          \"4\"\n        ],\n        \"heading\": \"Avatar Variety Mechanism\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"body\": \"The renderer is paired with simple environmental state. Regular floor tiles encode collision and shadow information, giving agents local movement decisions and preventing occupied-destination conflicts. The impostor database is also reused to project human shadows, which adds environmental visual integration at only one extra polygon per avatar shadow.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n          \"5\",\n          \"6\"\n        ],\n        \"heading\": \"Behavior and Shadows\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"body\": \"The reported results support the system-level claim but should be read with their constraints. On an 800-MHz Pentium III with a GeForce GTS2, the system displays 10,000 humans in a 41,260-polygon village at 12 to 20 frames per second. The frame-rate series also shows that the static city already costs much of the budget and that crowd scaling is close to linear in time per frame.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n          \"7\",\n          \"0\"\n        ],\n        \"heading\": \"Benchmark Interpretation\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"body\": \"The method assumes viewers remain far enough from avatars for orthographic impostors and limited texture dimensions to hold up. It also assumes effectively sun-like lighting for shadow reuse and relies on synthetic models rather than captured humans. The authors identify hybrid polygonal close-ups, normal-map shading, culling, photographic acquisition, and richer behavior as future improvements.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n          \"1\",\n          \"6\",\n          \"7\"\n        ],\n        \"heading\": \"Assumptions and Limits\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"summary_long\": \"The article addresses a specific graphics bottleneck: populated virtual cities require both large static environments and many animated human agents, but fully polygonal crowds make real-time display expensive. Tecchia, Loscos, and Chrysanthou therefore use image-based impostors for distant humans, exploiting the typical viewing conditions of urban walkthroughs where many people are visible but not necessarily close to the camera. Their approach is not just a generic billboard substitution. They tune the impostor representation for walking human geometry, reduce the texture-memory footprint of prerendered samples, and use multipass rendering to create visual variety from a limited set of human models. The technical path is pragmatic: reduce the number of polygons per person, retain enough directional and animation sampling to avoid severe artifacts, and spend saved memory on more types and appearances. Several pieces of evidence anchor the proposal. A PCA-informed plane-selection argument explains how the system reduces viewpoint-change popping without fully adopting a distortion-prone best-fitting plane. Packing each sample into its smallest rectangle cuts memory to 25 percent of the original sample layout, and OpenGL texture compression further reduces frame storage while retaining alpha data for later recoloring. Alpha-channel masks then support multipass rendering, changing clothes and other body regions while preserving the baked shading in the source texture. The implementation also integrates a grid for collision and shadowing, and reuses impostor silhouettes to create posture-aware shadows with one additional polygon. The benchmark demonstrates the intended scale: six avatar types, 10,000 different humans, a 41,260-polygon village, and measured frame rates that remain interactive despite falling as crowd size grows. The source is strongest as a systems paper showing how several approximate techniques combine into a usable real-time renderer; its limits are equally clear, including assumptions about camera distance, sunlight-like illumination, synthetic source models, and the need for culling and richer behavior in future work.\",\n    \"summary_short\": \"This source presents an image-based rendering pipeline for animated crowds in virtual cities. It combines optimized impostors, texture packing, compression, alpha-guided recoloring, and grid-based behavior support. The implementation reports 10,000 varied humans in a 41,260-polygon village at interactive frame rates.\"\n  },\n  \"source_extraction\": {\n    \"converter_name\": \"pdftotext\",\n    \"converter_version\": null,\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
